// Copilot CLI user settings template (JSONC: comments are allowed here).
//
// Installs to ~/.copilot/settings.json. This is the ONLY Copilot CLI config
// file that is meant to be hand-edited or templated. Two siblings in the same
// directory are auto-managed by the CLI and must never be templated, copied,
// or hand-edited:
//
//     ~/.copilot/config.json               auto-managed
//     ~/.copilot/permissions-config.json   auto-managed
//
// WHY THIS FILE IS ALMOST EMPTY. The active object below sets nothing on
// purpose. The permissions schema inside settings.json is documented in prose
// rather than as a published schema, so every key that would be worth setting
// here is UNVERIFIED. Shipping an unverified key as active config risks a
// parse failure on a machine you do not control, and a settings file that
// fails to parse is worse than one that says nothing. The harness's real
// wiring does not live here anyway: the guard hooks are wired in
// ~/.copilot/hooks/harness-hooks.json, which the installer writes.
//
// Everything below is a commented recipe. Uncomment a block only after you
// have confirmed the key against your installed CLI version, and re-validate
// the file afterwards.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// UNVERIFIED (prose-documented only, confirm before enabling):
//
//   "disableAllHooks": false
//       Kill switch for every hook. Leaving it unset is equivalent to false.
//       Set it to true only to debug whether a hook is causing a problem, and
//       put it back. Note it may be nested under "permissions" rather than
//       top-level, depending on CLI version.
//
//   "permissions": {
//     "disableBypassPermissionsMode": true
//         Refuses the CLI's bypass-permissions mode outright. On a managed
//         machine this is usually already forced by managed-settings.json.
//   }
//
//   "sandbox": { ... }
//       Sandboxed execution for tool calls. Shape not published; leave alone
//       until it is.
//
// DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and this is a decision rather than an omission:
//
//   No "model" key. The organization may pin the model list, and a pinned
//   model that this file contradicts is a confusing failure. Take whatever
//   the account gives you and check it with /model.
//
//   No bypass or auto-approve mode of any kind. This machine should keep
//   prompting for permission.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// DENY RULES ARE COMMAND-LINE FLAGS, NOT SETTINGS KEYS.
//
// The Claude Code half of this harness ships a permissions.deny list in its
// settings.json. The Copilot CLI equivalent is the --deny-tool grammar, which
// is verified. Deny always beats allow. The grammar is:
//
//     shell(git commit)      one exact command
//     shell(git:*)           a command prefix
//     write(path)            a write to a path
//     ServerName(tool)       an MCP tool on a named server
//
// The destructive-command set the Claude template denies, expressed as CLI
// flags. Wrap this in a shell alias or a wrapper script so you do not retype
// it, and note that it is defense in depth: the block-destructive-bash.sh
// guard hook already covers the same ground and gives better error messages.
//
//     copilot \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(rm:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(sudo:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(mkfs:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(dd:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(git push:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(git reset:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'shell(git clean:*)' \
//       --deny-tool 'write(~/.ssh)' \
//       --deny-tool 'write(~/.bashrc)' \
//       --deny-tool 'write(~/.zshrc)'
//
// Credential paths are worth denying on the READ side too, but the read-deny
// grammar is not documented the way write() is. Until it is, the honest
// mitigation is not to point the agent at those directories, and to keep
// ~/.ssh, ~/.aws, ~/.config/gh, and the keychain out of any repository the
// agent works in.
//
// After any edit here, validate with a JSONC-aware parser. Plain `jq` will
// reject this file because of these comments, which is expected.
{
}
